{
  "dom_id": "step-q344945",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193353/assets/f9ecaff65364279019c1cd503fd1c211941cb0022e44bc6f7fdda93a98bd7f34.png)\n\nThe graph of the first derivative $y={f}^{′}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-5,$ $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ and $x=7,$ as shown above. Find all the values of $x$ where the slope of the function $y=f(x)$ is positive.\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)⋃(2,$\n{{field-3}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is equal to zero on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is positive on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is negative on $(a,b).$\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-5,-1)$ and $(2,7):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193353/assets/1099479c33903078a3e8223fd5635f40cfc85daf045e46e07886a06c3a1bdbe7.png)\n\nTherefore, the slope of the function $f(x)$ is positive when\n\n$$\nx\\in (\\,-5,-1\\,)⋃(2,7\\,).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
